1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,483
This guy was so sick of paying
Adobe's cancellation fees…

2
00:00:04,550 --> 00:00:06,550
that he rebuilt it all.

3
00:00:06,617 --> 00:00:09,636
Photoshop, Premiere Pro,
Lightroom: seven free programs,

4
00:00:09,702 --> 00:00:14,323
released in under three days,
and an AI wrote the code.

5
00:00:14,390 --> 00:00:18,500
I tried making this video's
thumbnail with his Photoshop.

6
00:00:18,640 --> 00:00:22,200
If it works, honestly,
goodbye Adobe subscription.

7
00:00:22,267 --> 00:00:25,033
If not, it's the most
overhyped thing of the year.

8
00:00:25,100 --> 00:00:29,833
And in this story, Robin Hood has
a subscription to sell us too.

9
00:00:29,900 --> 00:00:32,073
We really are living in crazy times.

10
00:00:32,140 --> 00:00:35,033
Since Claude Opus 5.5 came out,

11
00:00:35,100 --> 00:00:37,993
people keep shipping
crazier and crazier stuff.

12
00:00:38,060 --> 00:00:41,913
On September 30th, a developer
from Atlanta, Brandon Thomas,

13
00:00:41,980 --> 00:00:44,650
starts a new project
on GitHub: PhotoCraft.

14
00:00:44,717 --> 00:00:47,293
The next evening,
he posts this on Hacker News,

15
00:00:47,360 --> 00:00:48,823
developers' favorite forum.

16
00:00:48,890 --> 00:00:51,293
He says, and I quote: "Software is over.

17
00:00:51,360 --> 00:00:56,263
But for real. I just one-shotted
Photoshop. The whole thing."

18
00:00:56,330 --> 00:01:01,000
And he adds:
"Our career is worthless soon."

19
00:01:02,210 --> 00:01:04,873
Honestly, I'm a developer myself,

20
00:01:04,940 --> 00:01:09,033
and my first thought
went to small SaaS tools:

21
00:01:09,100 --> 00:01:11,833
online services that do just one thing,

22
00:01:11,900 --> 00:01:15,033
like generating a QR code,
for example, or I don't know…

23
00:01:15,100 --> 00:01:18,090
maybe iLovePDF.

24
00:01:18,157 --> 00:01:22,433
If an AI can rebuild Photoshop in
three days, will all of that disappear?

25
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:25,890
Adobe included. We'll come
back to that at the end.

26
00:01:25,957 --> 00:01:30,683
In under three weeks, his project
got nearly 67,000 stars on GitHub.

27
00:01:30,750 --> 00:01:33,483
Stars are basically developers' likes.

28
00:01:33,550 --> 00:01:35,883
So, can you actually use it,

29
00:01:35,950 --> 00:01:38,523
or is it an empty shell?

30
00:01:38,590 --> 00:01:41,180
To answer that,
we have to go back to a bill.

31
00:01:41,760 --> 00:01:45,783
If you've ever had an Adobe subscription,
you might know the trap.

32
00:01:45,850 --> 00:01:50,173
You take the annual plan,
paid monthly: it looks flexible.

33
00:01:50,240 --> 00:01:52,893
Except if you leave during the first year,

34
00:01:52,960 --> 00:01:58,333
Adobe charges a cancellation fee:
half of what you have left to pay.

35
00:01:58,400 --> 00:02:03,133
In 2024, the US Justice Department
sued Adobe over these practices.

36
00:02:03,200 --> 00:02:08,013
In March 2026, Adobe agreed
to pay 150 million dollars,

37
00:02:08,080 --> 00:02:10,003
without admitting any wrongdoing.

38
00:02:10,070 --> 00:02:13,363
Brandon Thomas is one
of the people who paid.

39
00:02:13,430 --> 00:02:16,803
He wrote it on Reddit, and I quote:
"They charged me the cancellation fee

40
00:02:16,870 --> 00:02:21,140
on multiple occasions,
and I've hated them ever since."

41
00:02:21,240 --> 00:02:23,103
"On multiple occasions," he said.

42
00:02:23,170 --> 00:02:26,543
So he decided to farm
some aura off Adobe's back

43
00:02:26,610 --> 00:02:28,863
by rebuilding the whole company.

44
00:02:28,930 --> 00:02:31,823
We've all wanted revenge on Adobe.

45
00:02:31,890 --> 00:02:36,463
I pay €33 just to make thumbnails.

46
00:02:36,530 --> 00:02:40,050
He straight up cloned Photoshop.

47
00:02:40,280 --> 00:02:42,612
But hey, quick break: on Technovore,

48
00:02:42,679 --> 00:02:45,743
we break down the hidden
genius behind some of the apps

49
00:02:45,810 --> 00:02:47,263
you use every day.

50
00:02:47,330 --> 00:02:50,463
If that's your thing,
subscribe, it's free.

51
00:02:50,530 --> 00:02:52,240
And unlike Adobe…

52
00:02:52,470 --> 00:02:54,750
you can leave whenever you want.

53
00:02:55,120 --> 00:02:58,823
On Reddit, where his posts
are blowing up, he promises:

54
00:02:58,890 --> 00:03:02,663
"100% parity within a month."

55
00:03:02,730 --> 00:03:05,223
Translation: all of Photoshop in a month.

56
00:03:05,290 --> 00:03:08,263
Honestly, seven programs in three days,

57
00:03:08,330 --> 00:03:12,490
all of Photoshop promised in a month:
on paper, that's interesting.

58
00:03:12,557 --> 00:03:16,533
But paper doesn't make thumbnails,
and it's not everything.

59
00:03:16,600 --> 00:03:18,920
So I ran a test.

60
00:03:19,610 --> 00:03:21,613
Just one, a tiny little test.

61
00:03:21,680 --> 00:03:26,620
The challenge for me was making this
video's thumbnail with PhotoCraft.

62
00:03:26,687 --> 00:03:31,060
Basically, my real job,
the one I do for every video.

63
00:03:31,160 --> 00:03:36,203
But I'd like to go even further:
since this software was built for AIs,

64
00:03:36,270 --> 00:03:37,643
I can describe the style I want,

65
00:03:37,710 --> 00:03:42,090
give references, and let my AI
do the whole thing by itself.

66
00:03:42,450 --> 00:03:44,810
But hey, let's not push it.

67
00:03:45,080 --> 00:03:49,583
First, installation: there's
a version for Windows, Mac and Linux.

68
00:03:49,650 --> 00:03:54,703
Linux, bro! Seriously,
what software even supports Linux?

69
00:03:54,770 --> 00:03:57,473
It's super rare. Even Adobe isn't there.

70
00:03:57,540 --> 00:04:00,353
Makes sense for them, because
what Adobe cares about is money.

71
00:04:00,420 --> 00:04:04,413
And when you look at Linux,
with like ten people using Linux,

72
00:04:04,480 --> 00:04:07,133
the market isn't
profitable for them at all.

73
00:04:07,200 --> 00:04:12,123
But imagine you have a Steam
Deck you use as your main PC,

74
00:04:12,190 --> 00:04:14,003
since it runs Linux.

75
00:04:14,070 --> 00:04:17,603
With PhotoCraft, you can literally use

76
00:04:17,670 --> 00:04:21,690
your gaming console as a real desktop PC.

77
00:04:21,830 --> 00:04:24,683
On top of that, the file is under 100 MB,

78
00:04:24,750 --> 00:04:27,990
and the software honestly
starts in two seconds.

79
00:04:28,310 --> 00:04:30,873
Adobe's other strength is its interface.

80
00:04:30,940 --> 00:04:34,840
People are used to it,
and changing habits is incredibly hard.

81
00:04:34,907 --> 00:04:39,013
Except here, this guy shows up
with almost the same interface,

82
00:04:39,080 --> 00:04:42,793
the same shortcuts, and on top
of that, it opens PSD files.

83
00:04:42,860 --> 00:04:45,753
Photoshop files. Photoshop files!

84
00:04:45,820 --> 00:04:47,740
Honestly, what more could you ask for?

85
00:04:48,050 --> 00:04:49,793
Honestly, for my thumbnail,

86
00:04:49,860 --> 00:04:52,693
I don't use any complicated
artistic effects:

87
00:04:52,760 --> 00:04:57,780
layers, a cutout, some text,
and for me, that's all there is.

88
00:04:58,200 --> 00:05:00,543
Now, the real moment of truth:

89
00:05:00,610 --> 00:05:05,263
if I open an old Photoshop file from
one of the channel's thumbnails,

90
00:05:05,330 --> 00:05:06,543
a PSD file…

91
00:05:06,610 --> 00:05:10,143
the project admits it itself:
of files created by Photoshop,

92
00:05:10,210 --> 00:05:13,503
it only displays about half correctly.

93
00:05:13,570 --> 00:05:15,423
So, what about mine? What do you think?

94
00:05:15,490 --> 00:05:17,040
Go on, place your bets.

95
00:05:18,380 --> 00:05:22,163
For me, it opened my PSD file perfectly.

96
00:05:22,230 --> 00:05:24,323
I even made a few edits:

97
00:05:24,390 --> 00:05:30,323
I added Claude's logo to
make this video's thumbnail.

98
00:05:30,390 --> 00:05:36,340
And honestly, even launching
the software was super fast.

99
00:05:36,570 --> 00:05:39,153
Actually, since everything
is written in Rust,

100
00:05:39,220 --> 00:05:42,553
Rust being a language
known for being ultra fast

101
00:05:42,620 --> 00:05:45,340
and not using a lot of resources,

102
00:05:45,930 --> 00:05:51,240
PhotoCraft is super, super fast,
and starts in a heartbeat.

103
00:05:51,520 --> 00:05:55,223
And honestly, for my thumbnails,
it more than does the job.

104
00:05:55,290 --> 00:05:59,720
And for software released less than
three weeks ago, it's mind-blowing.

105
00:05:59,910 --> 00:06:03,623
But how does a single
developer ship seven programs

106
00:06:03,690 --> 00:06:05,880
this big in three days?

107
00:06:05,990 --> 00:06:10,813
Actually, Brandon has 15 years in
the trade, about ten of them in Rust.

108
00:06:10,880 --> 00:06:13,293
But in February, he made a radical choice.

109
00:06:13,360 --> 00:06:16,813
Quote: "I stopped writing
code entirely in February."

110
00:06:16,880 --> 00:06:21,933
Since then, the AI writes:
vanilla Claude Code, with Opus 5.5.

111
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:26,333
And across the seven apps,
that's almost 2.9 million lines of code.

112
00:06:26,400 --> 00:06:30,253
He plays site foreman:
he splits up the work,

113
00:06:30,320 --> 00:06:32,813
runs agents in parallel, reviews,

114
00:06:32,880 --> 00:06:36,093
steers the ship, with one rule
repeated in every project:

115
00:06:36,160 --> 00:06:38,590
never look at Adobe's code.

116
00:06:39,100 --> 00:06:43,023
That's called a clean-room
reimplementation.

117
00:06:43,090 --> 00:06:47,103
Basically, imagine a chef who
tastes a dish at a restaurant,

118
00:06:47,170 --> 00:06:48,883
then recreates it at home.

119
00:06:48,950 --> 00:06:50,623
He's allowed to taste the dish;

120
00:06:50,690 --> 00:06:53,123
he's not allowed to steal the recipe book.

121
00:06:53,190 --> 00:06:55,930
Here, the recipe book is Adobe's code.

122
00:06:55,997 --> 00:06:58,163
The project even bans decompiling,

123
00:06:58,230 --> 00:07:03,750
and relies on public documentation,
like the specs for PSD and PDF.

124
00:07:04,320 --> 00:07:06,583
Speaking of PDF, we need to talk about it:

125
00:07:06,650 --> 00:07:11,243
Adobe invented it, and with Acrobat,
practically privatized it.

126
00:07:11,310 --> 00:07:13,763
Yet it's been an open standard since 2008:

127
00:07:13,830 --> 00:07:16,830
even your browser can read a PDF.

128
00:07:17,900 --> 00:07:21,150
ArtCraft took the chance to
release its own PdfCraft.

129
00:07:21,360 --> 00:07:24,603
Another detail: every action
in these programs is a command.

130
00:07:24,670 --> 00:07:27,143
So an AI can drive them.

131
00:07:27,210 --> 00:07:30,363
You tell your Claude, or your GPT,

132
00:07:30,430 --> 00:07:35,050
"cut out this character," and it
does the clicking in PhotoCraft.

133
00:07:35,117 --> 00:07:38,603
I tried it myself to put
together my thumbnail,

134
00:07:38,670 --> 00:07:42,510
and it managed to do everything I asked.

135
00:07:43,220 --> 00:07:48,043
Except AI agents coding day
and night still cost a lot.

136
00:07:48,110 --> 00:07:51,243
So I looked into who's
actually paying for all this.

137
00:07:51,310 --> 00:07:53,900
And I landed on the pricing page.

138
00:07:54,280 --> 00:07:58,783
The apps really are free,
with no account needed.

139
00:07:58,850 --> 00:08:01,983
But they're published by a company called…

140
00:08:02,050 --> 00:08:04,623
ArtCraft. And ArtCraft, originally,

141
00:08:04,690 --> 00:08:08,170
is an AI image and
video generation studio.

142
00:08:08,237 --> 00:08:09,170
A paid one.

143
00:08:09,400 --> 00:08:13,920
With credits, and plans from
8 to 60 dollars a month.

144
00:08:14,100 --> 00:08:18,403
That's right: the man who cloned
Adobe out of hatred for subscriptions

145
00:08:18,470 --> 00:08:20,323
funds his free software…

146
00:08:20,390 --> 00:08:22,310
with subscriptions.

147
00:08:22,830 --> 00:08:26,613
Okay, to be fair:
his subscription can be canceled anytime.

148
00:08:26,680 --> 00:08:29,653
No exit fees, no trap.

149
00:08:29,720 --> 00:08:33,653
And ArtCraft doesn't stop
at Adobe: thirteen apps,

150
00:08:33,720 --> 00:08:37,670
with clones of Word, Excel,
PowerPoint, and even Figma.

151
00:08:37,737 --> 00:08:43,240
Honestly, like Thanos with the
Infinity Stones: he wants them all.

152
00:08:43,510 --> 00:08:47,133
Behind it, there's
a co-founder and a small team,

153
00:08:47,200 --> 00:08:48,733
with over 300 contributors.

154
00:08:48,800 --> 00:08:51,513
We're far from the legend of
the lone guy in his bedroom

155
00:08:51,580 --> 00:08:52,713
who's developing,

156
00:08:52,780 --> 00:08:57,580
or fighting Adobe single-handedly,
David-versus-Goliath style.

157
00:08:58,010 --> 00:09:01,513
In 2020, Brandon was already
saying: "I'm not a lawyer,

158
00:09:01,580 --> 00:09:05,131
but I think we're entering
into a legal gray area."

159
00:09:05,198 --> 00:09:08,913
That was about Vocodes, his site that made

160
00:09:08,980 --> 00:09:13,400
celebrity voices talk with AI,
now called FakeYou.

161
00:09:13,467 --> 00:09:17,003
In 2022, Italy's privacy watchdog

162
00:09:17,070 --> 00:09:19,243
opened an investigation into FakeYou.

163
00:09:19,310 --> 00:09:22,830
Today, the same question
is back with Photoshop:

164
00:09:23,300 --> 00:09:27,710
is cloning software with an AI legal?

165
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:30,423
On the code side,
the clean room protects him.

166
00:09:30,490 --> 00:09:33,943
But the menus and shortcuts
are deliberately the same,

167
00:09:34,010 --> 00:09:36,023
and that's exactly what makes it strong.

168
00:09:36,090 --> 00:09:37,143
As I'm recording this,

169
00:09:37,210 --> 00:09:41,640
Adobe has made no public statement,
and there's no known complaint.

170
00:09:42,270 --> 00:09:43,613
I'm not a lawyer either.

171
00:09:43,680 --> 00:09:46,833
But in my opinion, these copies
stay open source and free,

172
00:09:46,900 --> 00:09:48,673
with nothing to sell inside.

173
00:09:48,740 --> 00:09:52,660
And that protects them from
pretty much any legal attack.

174
00:09:52,727 --> 00:09:55,813
By the way, if you've seen
an "Adobe Reacts" video,

175
00:09:55,880 --> 00:09:58,913
watch out: it's a parody,
played by an actor.

176
00:09:58,980 --> 00:10:02,480
The question is whether
Brandon's promise holds up.

177
00:10:03,000 --> 00:10:04,883
100% of Photoshop in a month.

178
00:10:04,950 --> 00:10:09,423
Two days later, on Hacker News,
the promise had already shrunk:

179
00:10:09,490 --> 00:10:12,063
"It'll be measured in months, not years."

180
00:10:12,130 --> 00:10:15,823
And the project publishes its own
assessment, a pretty honest one:

181
00:10:15,890 --> 00:10:20,510
about 76% of Photoshop's
features exist in PhotoCraft.

182
00:10:20,610 --> 00:10:23,133
But ready for real professional work?

183
00:10:23,200 --> 00:10:25,240
About 45%.

184
00:10:25,330 --> 00:10:27,293
And to get all the way to Photoshop,

185
00:10:27,360 --> 00:10:33,460
it estimates another 2,300 to
4,100 hours of AI agent work.

186
00:10:33,527 --> 00:10:37,343
The first half of these apps,
the AI wrote in a few days.

187
00:10:37,410 --> 00:10:41,103
The second half is details:
a file that opens exactly the same,

188
00:10:41,170 --> 00:10:43,103
a color that doesn't shift when printed,

189
00:10:43,170 --> 00:10:46,320
a brush that responds the
way your hand expects.

190
00:10:46,530 --> 00:10:48,793
So, empty shell or not?

191
00:10:48,860 --> 00:10:52,713
Honestly, no. For a use like mine,
it's more than enough.

192
00:10:52,780 --> 00:10:56,393
Or for someone who used, say,
Photopea online, it's enough.

193
00:10:56,460 --> 00:10:59,113
But for a professional,
features are still missing:

194
00:10:59,180 --> 00:11:04,473
on Reddit and Hacker News,
users list them one by one.

195
00:11:04,540 --> 00:11:07,353
But over 300 contributors in three weeks

196
00:11:07,420 --> 00:11:10,540
shows that people really
care about the project.

197
00:11:10,607 --> 00:11:12,553
So, what about my job?

198
00:11:12,620 --> 00:11:14,793
Back to my small SaaS tools.

199
00:11:14,860 --> 00:11:18,473
A service that does just one thing,
like generating a QR code,

200
00:11:18,540 --> 00:11:23,370
I think it's going to disappear:
an AI can recreate it too easily.

201
00:11:23,437 --> 00:11:27,063
But a big company like Adobe
doesn't just sell the product:

202
00:11:27,130 --> 00:11:31,640
it also sells support,
and compatibility with years of projects.

203
00:11:32,400 --> 00:11:36,103
Anyway, I'm going to keep
using it for my thumbnails.

204
00:11:36,170 --> 00:11:40,183
Software under 100 MB that
starts in two seconds…

205
00:11:40,250 --> 00:11:41,863
honestly, you can't beat that.

206
00:11:41,930 --> 00:11:46,103
Just for handling thumbnails,
it's fine: it's a must-have for me.

207
00:11:46,170 --> 00:11:48,223
For you, it all depends on what you need.

208
00:11:48,290 --> 00:11:51,983
So tell me in the comments:
what would it take for you to drop Adobe?

209
00:11:52,050 --> 00:11:53,603
Or "I already use GIMP"?

210
00:11:53,670 --> 00:11:56,743
Up to you, no judgment here.

211
00:11:56,810 --> 00:11:59,210
And if you want more, subscribe.

212
00:11:59,277 --> 00:12:02,623
If you like understanding
how apps work under the hood,

213
00:12:02,690 --> 00:12:06,903
see you again for a big
video coming to the channel.

214
00:12:06,970 --> 00:12:09,150
Alright, bye, this was Inno.

